[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 7:21] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years.

[candidate · 8:51] Let me run it. Beans were 25% of 42%, so 10.5 points of revenue; other inputs 75%, so 31.5 points. Beans up 40% gives 14.7. Other inputs up 37.5% gives 43.3. Add those: 58.0. That's exactly today's COGS number.

So the entire 16-point move is explained by input price inflation, and it's broad — not just beans. Beans are only about a quarter of the basket, so even a 40% spike there is 4 points of the 16. The other 12 points are dairy, packaging, and food.

Which honestly reinforces my point: you can't contract-negotiate your way out of a 37.5% increase across every input category. The only lever that scales is price. Flat menu for two years against that kind of inflation is the root cause.

So — raise prices 7-10%, renegotiate where you can on the bean side, and that's the plan.
