[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 8:56] Okay. You've said 7-10% on menu prices. Walk me through how you got that range, and what it would do to the margin.

[candidate · 10:26] Honestly, the 7-10% was a judgment call on what customers would absorb without walking, not something I derived. Let me actually run it.

Costs are 94% of revenue. If I raise prices x% and volume holds, cost dollars stay flat, so margin = 1 − 94/(1+x). At 7%: 94/1.07 = 87.9, so margin 12.1%. At 10%: 94/1.1 = 85.5, margin 14.5%.

So my range gets us to roughly 12-14%, not back to 24%. To fully recover, I'd need 94/0.76 = 123.7, a 23.7% price increase. That's a lot more than I assumed, and at that level elasticity stops being a rounding error.

Do we have transaction volume or any elasticity data? Without it I'd assume volume holds flat, which is generous.

But the direction is unchanged — price is the lever, it just has to be bigger or paired with cost work.
