[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:31] I don't have elasticity data or a transaction volume breakdown. Beyond price increases and supplier renegotiation, what else could Brew & Bean do to close the remaining gap?

[candidate · 12:01] Fair — I'll assume volume holds, which is generous, and work the remainder.

A few things. Menu mix: shift customers toward higher-margin items. Brewed coffee and espresso have a much lower input load than dairy-heavy drinks, and dairy is in the bucket that drove most of the 12 points. Pushing black coffee, oat alternatives if cheaper, and food attach could move the blended COGS ratio without a headline price change.

Portion and waste control: at 200 locations, standardizing pours and cutting spoilage on dairy and food is real money when inputs are up 37.5%.

Packaging — it's inside that same bucket. Cheaper cups and lids, or a discount for reusables, is a straight cost line.

Overhead is only up 2 points, so I wouldn't spend time there.

But I'd be clear with the CEO: these are supporting moves. Flat prices for two years against this inflation is the cause, and price is the primary fix.
