[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:03] Okay. Pricing is the primary lever in your view. Which of your ideas would you sequence first, and what risk to the business would you watch as you roll them out?

[candidate · 13:33] I'd sequence price first, but staged rather than one 24% jump. Start with a 7-8% increase, which gets us to roughly 12% margin, and run it in a subset of locations — say 20 stores — so we can actually measure volume response, since we don't have elasticity data. That pilot generates the data we're missing. Then a second increase a few quarters later based on what we learn.

In parallel, because they're fast and invisible to customers: waste and portion control, and packaging substitution. Those don't carry demand risk.

Menu mix third — it takes merchandising work and takes longer to show up.

Main risk is volume loss. Specialty coffee has substitutes, and if traffic drops more than a few percent the price increase is self-defeating. Second risk is that input prices mean-revert; if beans fall back we'd be over-priced versus competitors who held. So I'd watch transaction counts weekly and keep the second increase contingent on the pilot.
