[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 13:37] You said a traffic drop of more than a few percent would make the price increase self-defeating. Walk me through how you'd work out that threshold.

[candidate · 15:07] Let me set it up. Split costs into variable and fixed. COGS at 58 moves with volume. Labor 22 and overhead 14 are mostly fixed in the short run — stores are staffed and leased regardless. So 36 points fixed.

Take revenue 100 today, profit 6. Now raise price 7% and let v be the volume index. Revenue becomes 107v, COGS 58v, fixed stays 36. Profit = 49v − 36.

To hold today's 6 points of profit: 49v = 42, so v = 0.857. That's a 14% traffic drop before we're worse off than today.

To just break even on profit: 49v = 36, v = 0.735, a 26.5% drop.

So my "few percent" was too conservative — the cushion is more like 14%. Caveat: labor isn't perfectly fixed, you'd flex hours at the margin, which lowers the threshold somewhat. But 14% is the number I'd pilot against.
