[interviewer · WRAP · 15:11] Understood. Now the CEO is on the line and wants your final recommendation in under a minute. What do you tell them?

[candidate · 16:41] Brew & Bean's margin problem is almost entirely input cost inflation that you never passed through to customers. COGS went from 42% to 58% of revenue — that's 16 of the 18 points you lost. Labor is flat, overhead is up only 2. And menu prices haven't moved in two years while your input basket rose about 37.5%.

So: raise prices. I'd start at 7-8% across the menu, piloted in roughly 20 stores. That alone takes margin from 6% to about 12%, and the math says you'd need a 14% traffic drop before you're worse off than today — so the downside is well covered. Then a second increase based on what the pilot shows.

Alongside that, waste and portion control on dairy, cheaper packaging, and a push toward lower-input drinks and food attach.

Risks: volume loss, and input prices reverting and leaving you priced above competitors.

Next step is the pilot — it gives us the elasticity data we don't currently have.
